\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {1}Sketches of the System}{1}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Depicted is the $yz$-projection of the incline system, the $z$-direction is orthogonal to the paper plane. The incline has an inclination angle $\varphi $ and the sphere is of radius $R$. Two orthonormal coordinate frames are considered: The fixed frame $\{ \bm  {e}_x, \bm  {e}_y, \bm  {e}_z\}$ and the body frame $\{ \hat  {\bm  {e}}_x, \hat  {\bm  {e}}_y, \hat  {\bm  {e}}_z \}$. Vectors expressed in the body frame carry hats and are time-dependent, stationary vectors not. Blue vectors might have a non-zero $z$-component and are therefore only projections. The outer magnetic field is represented by $\bm  {B}$, the magnetic dipole vector of the sphere by $\hat  {\bm  {\mu }}(t)$, and the centre of mass measured from the fixed frame by $\bm  {r}(t)$. The in-plane angular velocities of the body frame are shown as $\hat  {\bm  {\omega }}_x$ and $\hat  {\bm  {\omega }}_y$.}}{1}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:ramp}{{1}{1}{}{figure.1}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Shown is a sketch of the flat system with sufficiently small initial displacement angle $\beta _0$ between the outer magnetic field $\bm  {B}$ and the initial magnetic dipole vector $\bm  {\mu }_0$. Note that the system is effectively reduced to two dimensions. The red vectors indicate the oscillation-driving electromagnetic force $\bm  {F}_\text  {em}$ and the damping frictional force $\bm  {F}_\text  {fric}$. The frictional force includes aerodynamical drag and rolling resistance.}}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:flat}{{2}{2}{}{figure.2}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {2}Figure Family A}{2}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A1}{\ignorespaces Shown are different evolutions of the $y$-projection of the centre-of-mass coordinate $\bm  {r}=(x,y,z)^\top $ over time $t$ for different incline angles $\varphi $ without friction. Lines were calculated from the analytical solution and compared to the numerical results, represented as dots.}}{3}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {A2/A3}{\ignorespaces The left plot shows the energy evolution $E(t)$ of the system over time $t$, while the right one depicts the $y$-COM-coordinate evolution. The data originates from the numerical simulation program. Compared are different friction models, where \emph  {Coulomb} represents dry rolling resistance, \emph  {viscous} represents a linear damping approximation to rolling resistance, and \emph  {aerodynamic} represents spherical aerodynamic drag.}}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {3}Figure Family B}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {4}Figure Family C}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {5}Figure Family D}{4}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {D1}{\ignorespaces The surface represents the $x$-value the sphere attains after $t=1 \, \text  {s}$ as a function of the initial $x$- and $y$-components of the magnetic dipole vector of the sphere. Since the $x$-direction is transverse to the rolling motion induced by gravity, it can be seen as a measure for the influence of the magnetic force on the trajectory. The graphs at the $\mu _y x$-plane are slices of the surface for fixed values of $\mu _x$, and respectively for the graphes on the $\mu _x x$-plane with fixed $\mu _y$.}}{5}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {D2}{\ignorespaces These plottings are enhanced versions of the slices of figure D1, left for different fixed $\mu _y$ and right for different fixed $\mu _x$. Keeping $\mu _y$ fixed produces symmetrical deviations from the free rolling path depending on sign and magnitude of $\mu _x$, as one would expect. A clear transition from regular sigmoid behaviour to non-smooth responses occures at highly negative values of $\mu _y$, at which the ball no longer rolls down, discussed more rigorously in D3. The slice graphs for fixed $\mu _x$ on the right show a characteristic constant curve for $\mu _x=0$, as there can be no deviation from the purely gravitational path without a torque non-parallel to the $yz$-plane. All other slices display an asymmetry for positive and negative values. This is explained by the fact that for positive $\mu _y$, the direction of rolling increases the misalignment of $\bm  {\mu }$ with $\bm  {B}$, slowing down the motion and hence also the deviation. On the other hand, negative $\mu _y$-values cause $\bm  {\mu }$ to align with $\bm  {B}$, accelerating the motion in general and hence also the deviation in particular. A less regular asymmetry occurs for larger negative $\mu _y$-values, which is again the critical region where the magnetic force dominates over and acts against gravity, causing looping paths instead of downward motion.}}{6}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {D3}{\ignorespaces Visible are different trajectories of the sphere on the $xy$-plane for selected $\mu _y$-slices and all $\mu _x$-values corresponding to these slices. Here, the anomaly in D1 and D2 for large negative $\mu _y$-values can be seen immediately. For smaller negative and all positive $\mu _y$-slices, $\mu _y$ only controls the spread of the trajectories, while $\mu _x$ influences the deviation from a straight path. At $\mu _y=-1$, the magnetic force induced by the $\mu _y$-component as soon as the $\mu _x$-induced torque bends the trajectory out of the $yz$-plane overcompensates gravity and causes an upward motion. After that, the sphere moves in roughly elliptic loops.}}{7}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {6}Figure Family E}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {E}{\ignorespaces The graph on the left shows the $y$-projection of the sphere's COM at $t=1 \, \text  {s}$ as a function of initial $\mu _z$-values. There are roughly three discernible regions: First, the $y$-value decreases, which is expected as the rolling motion increases the misalignment of $\bm  {\mu }$ with $\bm  {B}$. On the right, the $y$-trajectory for $\mu _z=0$ is representative for this region. For increasingly high $\mu _z$-values, a transition phase occurs. The representing graph at $\mu _z = 0.95$ shows that in this region, the sphere begins to oscillate slightly at first, but gravity soon overcomes the magnetic force. For $\mu _z \geq 0.96$, the magnetic force fully overcomes the gravitational force which only causes small oscillations around the origin, as seen by the representing graph for $\mu _z=1$.}}{8}{}\protected@file@percent }
